00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.949
And this edition of StarTalk,

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:30.880
we're calling Summer School. (laughs)

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.056
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:00:32.056 --> 00:00:33.235
- Who said yeah?

00:00:33.235 --> 00:00:34.550
- The professor. (laughs)

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.760
- Friend and colleague, Charles Liu.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:37.793
Charles, welcome.

00:00:38.887 --> 00:00:40.050
- Thank you so much for having
me, I love Summer School.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:40.960
- [Neil] You love Summer school.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.250
- Absolutely.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.160
- [Neil] And I got my cohost
for this episode, Matt Kirshen.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:44.993
Matt.

00:00:44.993 --> 00:00:46.440
- Hey.
- Welcome back.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:47.330
- Thank you so much.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.740
- All right, host of
Probably, Maybe Science.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.960
- Probably Science.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.110
- Could be Science?

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:53.943
- Nearly Science.

00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:54.776
- Not Sure if it's Science?

00:00:54.776 --> 00:00:55.946
- Almost there.

00:00:55.946 --> 00:00:56.779
- (laughs) Almost the--

00:00:56.779 --> 00:00:57.859
- I Can't Believe it's Not Science/

00:00:57.859 --> 00:00:58.756
(laughs)

00:00:58.756 --> 00:00:59.589
Noisy Science.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:00.522
- Probably Science.

00:01:00.522 --> 00:01:04.810
A weekly review of science current events.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:05.643
- That's exactly it.

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:06.630
- [Neil] Excellent, excellent.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.308
- That's the podcast.

00:01:08.308 --> 00:01:09.141
- [Neil] Keep that goin', keep that goin'.

00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:10.510
- [Neil] Charles, professor.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:11.699
- Yes.

00:01:11.699 --> 00:01:12.532
- [Neil] At City University of New York--

00:01:12.532 --> 00:01:13.933
- So we're kind of equals here.

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:15.211
(laughs)

00:01:15.211 --> 00:01:16.137
- Absolutely.

00:01:16.137 --> 00:01:16.970
No, we are.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:17.803
Peas in a pod.

00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:19.230
- This is a Cosmic Queries version,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.920
but it's called Summer School.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.530
People just tryin' to catch up--

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.363
- That's right.

00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:25.560
- [Neil] With stuff they might've missed.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.213
- Or get ahead.

00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:28.046
- Or get ahead.

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:29.600
I forgot, summer school's
for gettin' ahead, too.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.920
For motivated students.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.670
- And with that in mind, I think,

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.260
let's start off with a quite

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.010
an academically advanced question.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.151
- Let's do it.

00:01:38.151 --> 00:01:38.984
- Ooh.

00:01:38.984 --> 00:01:40.587
- Cooper Holland on Instagram asks,

00:01:40.587 --> 00:01:42.757
"How hard would I have to fart to

00:01:42.757 --> 00:01:45.207
"knock Earth into escape velocity.

00:01:45.207 --> 00:01:48.506
"Also, wouldn't sunrise
be a good time to try it?"

00:01:48.506 --> 00:01:49.470
Mmm.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.710
That's Cooper Holland from Arizona State.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.504
- From ASU, huh.

00:01:53.504 --> 00:01:56.640
- I'm coughing because
I'm imagining how much gas

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.460
needs to be used to make that happen.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.890
But the reality is,
first I assume you have

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.570
an escape velocity from the Sun, right?

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.080
- No, he wants to escape from the Earth.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.470
- Oh, he wants to escape from the Earth.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.450
I thought he wanted the
Earth to escape from the Sun.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.630
- Oh, that's different.
- Now that is a massive--

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.180
- Yeah, it says to knock
Earth into escape velocity.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.078
- That's harder than just
if he wanted to escape.

00:02:17.078 --> 00:02:18.091
- Right, right.

00:02:18.091 --> 00:02:18.924
If he wanted to escape, that's easy.

00:02:18.924 --> 00:02:19.757
(laughs)

00:02:19.757 --> 00:02:20.898
- Just a few cans of beans--

00:02:20.898 --> 00:02:22.363
- [Charles] There you go.

00:02:22.363 --> 00:02:23.589
- We put him on a gantry. (laughs)

00:02:23.589 --> 00:02:24.932
- No, Earth would be a lot harder.

00:02:24.932 --> 00:02:28.268
Because Earth's orbital velocity--

00:02:28.268 --> 00:02:30.200
- Now I can't get that
picture out of my head.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.667
He's positioned on a
gantry at Cape Canavaral.

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:35.523
- Three, two, one--
- Last two cup of beans,

00:02:35.523 --> 00:02:37.880
he goes-- (laughs)

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.867
- Launch aborted for weather.

00:02:39.867 --> 00:02:40.987
- Ascends.

00:02:40.987 --> 00:02:42.570
- But is that solid fuel
or a liquid fuel rocket?

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:43.403
- Oh, yeah, no--

00:02:43.403 --> 00:02:44.912
- Yeah, see, see?

00:02:44.912 --> 00:02:47.002
- That would be, no that's
just air pressure fuel.

00:02:47.002 --> 00:02:48.172
- That's just the yeah--

00:02:48.172 --> 00:02:49.762
- That's just pressure fuel.

00:02:49.762 --> 00:02:50.905
- It's really a bottle rocket.

00:02:50.905 --> 00:02:51.738
(laughs)

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:52.571
- Pneumatic rocket.

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:53.725
- Pneumatic.

00:02:53.725 --> 00:02:56.472
- Or like a, the CO2 pellet rifle--

00:02:56.472 --> 00:02:58.965
- Yeah, right, like a, yeah, wow.

00:02:58.965 --> 00:03:01.600
No, see, Earth's orbital velocity is

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.918
about 30 kilometers per second, right?

00:03:03.918 --> 00:03:04.751
- Per second, correct.

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:06.253
- Around the Sun.

00:03:06.253 --> 00:03:08.526
You have to take the
square root of two of that,

00:03:08.526 --> 00:03:10.320
and so that makes 1.4 times 30,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.440
so about 42 kilometers a second.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.637
Earth's mass is about six--

00:03:14.637 --> 00:03:16.076
- 'Cause it's a cute little fact

00:03:16.076 --> 00:03:18.610
that whatever is your orbital velocity

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.610
around what you're orbiting,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.870
if you multiply that by
the square root of two,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.010
that's the speed to escape that object.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.048
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:03:25.048 --> 00:03:26.113
- That's a very cool fact.

00:03:26.113 --> 00:03:27.299
- It is, it's neat.

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:28.132
It's nice coincidence in math.

00:03:28.132 --> 00:03:30.519
And the Earth's mass of--

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:32.690
- Square root of two is about 1.4, yeah.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:37.006
- Earth's mass is six
billion trillion tons, right?

00:03:37.006 --> 00:03:41.528
So to generate the amount
of impulse necessary to get

00:03:41.528 --> 00:03:44.820
Earth, that much mass,
up to that velocity,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.350
that's a lot of cans of beans.

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.110
- Yeah, because you,

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.180
it's a momentum thing.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.859
So you need enough expelled gas

00:03:53.859 --> 00:03:58.036
so that the recoil of your
planet can be meaningful

00:03:58.036 --> 00:04:00.429
and significant.

00:04:00.429 --> 00:04:01.990
- And correct me if I'm
wrong, but you'd also have to

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.439
make sure you're in some
way tethered to the Earth

00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:05.784
so you're not just blasting yourself.

00:04:05.784 --> 00:04:06.840
- You can drag the Earth with you.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.899
- Right.

00:04:07.899 --> 00:04:09.510
- But otherwise you would just
fly off at a tangent, right.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:10.960
- [Charles] So I think--

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.399
- Ideally you'd want to get
all humans involved in this.

00:04:13.399 --> 00:04:14.232
- [Charles] Right.

00:04:14.232 --> 00:04:15.729
- You'd need every--
- you'd wanna be holding onto

00:04:15.729 --> 00:04:16.797
a tree or something.

00:04:16.797 --> 00:04:18.472
- You'd also need every
cow in the world, too.

00:04:18.472 --> 00:04:19.728
- [Neil] Yeah, the cows.

00:04:19.728 --> 00:04:20.770
- You gotta put 'em all together,

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.010
I think that's a very
summer school problem

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.240
calculation to do, yes.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.788
Cooper, good luck with that.

00:04:25.788 --> 00:04:26.621
(laughs)

00:04:26.621 --> 00:04:28.016
That's great.

00:04:28.016 --> 00:04:29.437
- But in principle, yes.

00:04:29.437 --> 00:04:32.099
So in other words, if you are in space,

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:35.321
the various ways you can actually
set yourself into motion,

00:04:35.321 --> 00:04:38.610
but to do so you have to
lose mass from your body.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.066
Either through gas, liquid, or solid.

00:04:42.066 --> 00:04:45.210
- And if you're at sunrise
that would be a good time,

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.680
because the best way to
inject that extra velocity,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.710
the delta V, is in the
direction of the orbit.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.020
The tangent to the orbit.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.140
So not straight out, as
some people might think,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.980
but actually out at a 90 degree angle.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.714
- So you can build to the speed that

00:04:58.714 --> 00:05:00.510
the Earth has itself, yes.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.010
- Right.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.620
Okay.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.970
- By the way, but all that methane,

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.420
that could be pretty dangerous situation.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.623
- It's true, one match
and you've got yeah--

00:05:09.623 --> 00:05:10.663
- Then you have a bomb.

00:05:10.663 --> 00:05:11.771
- That's a flare.

00:05:11.771 --> 00:05:13.330
- That's a bomb.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.090
- And also that's a greenhouse gas.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:15.923
- [Neil] Yes it is.

00:05:15.923 --> 00:05:17.330
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:18.404
- Yeah, very potent, more potent than CO2.

00:05:18.404 --> 00:05:19.237
- True.

00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:20.730
So you really have to light that thing.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:21.949
Yeah.

00:05:21.949 --> 00:05:22.951
(laughs)

00:05:22.951 --> 00:05:25.266
You would fly out into space.

00:05:25.266 --> 00:05:26.658
(crosstalk)

00:05:26.658 --> 00:05:27.910
- Now wait a minute.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:29.410
Wait, if you leave Earth,

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.172
you need the greenhouse gases

00:05:31.172 --> 00:05:32.005
to retain--

00:05:32.005 --> 00:05:33.346
- To stay warm.
- There you go.

00:05:33.346 --> 00:05:34.179
- The warmth.
- That's right.

00:05:34.179 --> 00:05:36.490
So, it's the balance,
it's the balance of, yeah.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.263
Wow, that is very exciting.

00:05:38.263 --> 00:05:40.279
You know what, I think I'm
gonna have to assign that

00:05:40.279 --> 00:05:42.670
to my astrophysics class next year.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.713
- And then the Dean will call you,

00:05:44.713 --> 00:05:46.636
(laughs) to the office and say--

00:05:46.636 --> 00:05:49.544
- Well, maybe Cooper will
help me out with that.

00:05:49.544 --> 00:05:52.577
- Well, while we are doing calculations,

00:05:52.577 --> 00:05:54.858
Tom Foreman on Facebook asks,

00:05:54.858 --> 00:05:58.199
"Is math a discovery or an invention?"

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:00.567
- Beautiful, beautiful.

00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:01.400
- Don't get me started.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.679
I wanna hear Charles first.

00:06:02.679 --> 00:06:05.070
- Okay, well, remember that
my wife is a mathematician.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.338
- [Neil] Yes, she is.

00:06:06.338 --> 00:06:07.171
- Right.

00:06:07.171 --> 00:06:08.685
- And my wife has a PhD
in Mathematical Physics.

00:06:08.685 --> 00:06:09.518
- [Charles] That's right.

00:06:09.518 --> 00:06:10.397
- Yeah.

00:06:10.397 --> 00:06:11.230
- Twinsies!

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:12.063
(laughs)

00:06:12.063 --> 00:06:13.560
- So we got math goin' in the family here.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.338
- Yeah, and my son also, given a choice of

00:06:16.338 --> 00:06:20.530
either studying math or
astrophysics in college

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.290
has chosen math.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.690
Anyway, that's okay, we still love you.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:25.523
- I'm sorry.

00:06:25.523 --> 00:06:26.933
- Yeah, I know, I know.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:29.740
- Such a disappointment.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:30.573
- When did you know?

00:06:30.573 --> 00:06:32.092
Were the signs always there?

00:06:32.092 --> 00:06:34.950
(laughs)

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.760
- When he was a little kid,
putting refrigerator magnets

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.153
in strange shapes, I knew
that something was up.

00:06:40.153 --> 00:06:40.986
- You just knew something--

00:06:40.986 --> 00:06:41.967
- Something was up.

00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:43.013
- He was hiding something.

00:06:43.013 --> 00:06:43.846
- Something was up.

00:06:43.846 --> 00:06:45.474
- In the closet, okay.

00:06:45.474 --> 00:06:50.474
- (sighs) anyway, math is
something that exists by itself,

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:56.370
but mathematics as we use
it and formulate it today

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.860
is an invention of humans.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.860
This is a clear reality of the universe.

00:07:05.499 --> 00:07:07.660
'Cause things will do what they're doing

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.480
whether we understand them or not,

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.440
and we created math in order to try to

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.670
understand and reproduce
and utilize those things

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.890
that nature provides for us.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:22.851
So, for example, when the
ancients were building pyramids,

00:07:22.851 --> 00:07:26.070
they invented geometry, right?

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.850
Those pyramids would've stood anyway,

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:30.320
if we had put them together
and not known the equations.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.450
But with the equations, the ancients were

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.850
able to build them properly.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.101
The same is true with,
say, rocket equations,

00:07:36.101 --> 00:07:38.660
that allow us to send
things out into space.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.160
We could've sent things into space

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.230
without knowing how to
do the calculations,

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.510
but we wouldn't have had
much control over it.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:48.740
So, bottom line, the math
you see in textbooks today,

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.198
or in papers, we humans have invented that

00:07:52.198 --> 00:07:54.438
following a set of rules that

00:07:54.438 --> 00:07:57.433
nature has provided for us as a template.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.310
Neil.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:00.143
- I'm okay with that.

00:08:00.143 --> 00:08:00.976
- [Charles] Okay.

00:08:00.976 --> 00:08:01.809
- I'm okay.

00:08:01.809 --> 00:08:04.870
I don't like debates about whether

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:08.250
one word or another
word best describes it.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.040
I'd rather say that maybe our language

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.120
needs yet a third word
that perfectly accounts

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:17.118
for it and then we get rid
of the argument altogether.

00:08:17.118 --> 00:08:18.121
- Oh.

00:08:18.121 --> 00:08:19.294
- And would that word be a
discovery or an invention?

00:08:19.294 --> 00:08:20.127
(laughs)

00:08:20.127 --> 00:08:22.070
- It's the word between
the two that we don't have.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.380
- Disco-vention.

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:24.474
- It's why we argue--

00:08:24.474 --> 00:08:26.880
- (singing) Mathematics, math math.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.310
Oh yeah, yeah, ya math.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.162
- Is it a particle or is it a wave in--

00:08:30.162 --> 00:08:31.636
why are we arguing that?

00:08:31.636 --> 00:08:33.860
It's both, we just don't have a word.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.810
We tried, we tried "wavicle,"
but it didn't catch on,

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.310
a wave and a particle.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.646
So, I just don't--

00:08:39.646 --> 00:08:42.930
- Sounds like some sort of
branding exercise from the 80s.

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.070
(laughs)

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:44.903
It's wavical.

00:08:44.903 --> 00:08:46.569
- 70s, discovention, okay?

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:49.161
- The fact that math works at all,

00:08:49.161 --> 00:08:51.730
as a tool to decode the universe,

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:52.860
is evidence

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.770
that the universe, at least the parts

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.653
that have revealed itself to us,

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:06.190
follows logical repeatable patterns.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:11.040
And math is simply a
way to code for logical,

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.190
repeatable patterns.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.580
So it's remarkable that math can

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.800
describe the universe at all,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.210
except that math is a
perfectly logical system,

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.400
and so is the universe.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:21.662
Put 'em together,

00:09:21.662 --> 00:09:23.017
of course,

00:09:23.017 --> 00:09:24.640
it's a marriage made in heaven.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.200
- Very much like music, right?

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.414
Math, music, that kind of connection,

00:09:28.414 --> 00:09:30.480
and the BeeGees understood that well,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.678
with their song

00:09:31.678 --> 00:09:35.947
(singing) Calculus, calculus,
yeah yeah yeah calculus!

00:09:36.945 --> 00:09:40.233
- I thought Calculus
was an emperor of Rome.

00:09:40.233 --> 00:09:41.534
(laughs)

00:09:41.534 --> 00:09:44.620
That's a old joke, I heard that long ago.

00:09:45.783 --> 00:09:47.210
- I'm gonna--

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:48.996
- [Neil] Calculus, the brother of Clavius.

00:09:48.996 --> 00:09:50.806
(laughs)

00:09:50.806 --> 00:09:51.646
Matt.

00:09:51.646 --> 00:09:53.204
- Well, while we're talking about things--

00:09:53.204 --> 00:09:54.700
- Get us off this topic really fast.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:55.533
- I will.

00:09:55.533 --> 00:09:57.039
Well I'm gonna combine two
different questions together.

00:09:57.039 --> 00:10:00.317
So Eric Hanson on Facebook asks,

00:10:00.317 --> 00:10:02.627
"I recently read that if all of the space

00:10:02.627 --> 00:10:04.147
"were taken out of every human on Earth,

00:10:04.147 --> 00:10:06.617
"the resulting mass would be about the

00:10:06.617 --> 00:10:08.367
"size of a cube of sugar."

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:10.388
- Yep, that's about right.

00:10:10.388 --> 00:10:11.221
- "How, then, can anyone
adequately explain

00:10:11.221 --> 00:10:13.508
"how the entire mass of
the observable universe

00:10:13.508 --> 00:10:17.493
"was at once a point only microns wide?"

00:10:17.493 --> 00:10:19.833
And then, I'm gonna combine this

00:10:19.833 --> 00:10:21.160
with this other question from--

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.615
- You're gonna combine that?

00:10:22.615 --> 00:10:23.650
- That's a beautiful question on its own.

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:25.290
- How you gonna add somethin' to that?

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.123
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:26.123 --> 00:10:27.300
- Well, this is a little coda question,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.070
I could've thrown it out afterwards,

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.449
but I thought I'm gonna leave this

00:10:31.449 --> 00:10:32.282
just in your subconscious to

00:10:32.282 --> 00:10:33.690
bubble over while you're
answering that one.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:36.047
From Ashton Norton, also on Facebook,

00:10:36.047 --> 00:10:38.417
"Other than the Big
Bang, are there any other

00:10:38.417 --> 00:10:39.727
"scientific theories that have been

00:10:39.727 --> 00:10:41.639
"discussed as possible explanations

00:10:41.639 --> 00:10:44.117
"for where we came from?"

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.423
So those two together, or
separate as you choose.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.860
- Okay, so this is basically
a cosmology question.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:53.693
- Sure.
- Right?

00:10:53.693 --> 00:10:56.590
So I'll take the cosmology
question, number one,

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.941
you can take cosmology
question number two.

00:10:58.941 --> 00:10:59.960
- I like both of 'em.

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.117
- All right--

00:11:01.117 --> 00:11:01.950
- No, I'm just kidding. (laughs)

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.805
- I'll answer each one half way.

00:11:03.805 --> 00:11:04.820
- Okay, you answer each half.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:05.803
Leave me a little room at the end, go.

00:11:05.803 --> 00:11:07.805
- All right, so.

00:11:07.805 --> 00:11:11.260
The reason the universe is so different

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.190
now than it was back then,

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:15.891
is simply because there was some kind of

00:11:15.891 --> 00:11:18.270
physics that happened between the

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.079
moment of the Big Bang and the present day

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:22.550
that we still don't yet understand.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.300
It is completely true that the

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:27.420
universe back then, the
density of the universe,

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.330
right around the moment of the Big Bang,

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:30.840
at the plank time, we call it,

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:35.840
was approximately 10 to the
97 kilograms per cubic meter.

00:11:36.214 --> 00:11:37.200
- That's pretty dense.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:38.033
- Yeah.

00:11:38.033 --> 00:11:38.971
And even if we took--

00:11:38.971 --> 00:11:39.804
- Heavy, man.

00:11:39.804 --> 00:11:41.960
- All of humanity-- (laughs)

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:43.390
We're back in the 60s now.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.189
We went from the 70s to the 60s--

00:11:45.189 --> 00:11:46.380
- Heavy.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.088
- Absolutely, you said it.

00:11:48.088 --> 00:11:53.088
We have a circumstance where
the matter of that density

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.290
is so removed from
anything we can think of,

00:11:56.290 --> 00:12:00.500
that human beings the size
of a cube of sugar analogy

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.360
when you move all the space,

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.370
that density is still
only about 10 to the 15

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:07.750
kilograms per cubic meter.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.023
- So there was just
another state of matter,

00:12:10.023 --> 00:12:11.765
or the matter itself--

00:12:11.765 --> 00:12:12.760
- Wait, wait Charles,

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.600
I thought it's not even
that complicated, okay?

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:15.433
- Okay.

00:12:15.433 --> 00:12:17.531
- If it stays as solid matter,

00:12:17.531 --> 00:12:20.181
just taking out all the
space between the particles,

00:12:20.181 --> 00:12:22.577
yeah, you're not gonna get
much smaller than that.

00:12:22.577 --> 00:12:25.352
But at the temperatures
of the early universe,

00:12:25.352 --> 00:12:28.276
matter is not stable as solid matter.

00:12:28.276 --> 00:12:30.760
It's energy.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.980
And you can pack energy into any kinda

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:34.555
small volume you want.

00:12:34.555 --> 00:12:36.042
So--

00:12:36.042 --> 00:12:37.148
- That's the right way to say it.

00:12:37.148 --> 00:12:38.174
- Is that a fair way to--

00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:39.346
- It's a completely fair way to say it.

00:12:39.346 --> 00:12:40.948
And so as a result, the laws of physics

00:12:40.948 --> 00:12:42.720
that were governing that behavior,

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:46.037
produced something that eventually

00:12:46.037 --> 00:12:48.750
evolved into our universe today.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:53.070
And we have yet to
decipher, scientifically,

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:55.403
the processes that went from that to now.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:58.060
- Wait, wait, I have to tighten that.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:59.190
It's not that it went from that to now,

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.010
it went from then to a
little bit after then,

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:03.860
because we know what happened a

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:04.969
little bit after then until now.

00:13:04.969 --> 00:13:05.802
We got that.

00:13:05.802 --> 00:13:07.594
- That's fair.
- That's good physics.

00:13:07.594 --> 00:13:08.930
- And by little bit after, Neil,

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:11.110
I think you're referring
to 10 to the minus

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:14.650
- 30th seconds, yeah.
- 30th seconds, right?

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.679
0.000000000.

00:13:16.679 --> 00:13:17.916
- A gazillionth of a second.

00:13:17.916 --> 00:13:18.916
(laughs)

00:13:18.916 --> 00:13:20.630
- So we've managed to trace
it back a very long way.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.053
- Yes, but that last little bit--

00:13:22.053 --> 00:13:24.590
- Just that last
gazillionth of a second is

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.017
the really confusing bit.

00:13:26.017 --> 00:13:29.240
- And that segues into that
second part of the question.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:30.470
- [Neil] It does, right.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.479
- What is going on in that
tiny, tiny fraction of a second

00:13:33.479 --> 00:13:36.357
that is so different from what we

00:13:36.357 --> 00:13:38.983
know today in our universe?

00:13:38.983 --> 00:13:41.906
And so that's where the
speculation can lie.

00:13:41.906 --> 00:13:44.320
What could've happened other than a

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.429
Big Bang as we understand it today.

00:13:46.429 --> 00:13:49.271
Could something else have generated the

00:13:49.271 --> 00:13:53.280
kinds of energies and effects that have

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.990
led to the way the universe expands today.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:58.781
There's lots of speculation,
it was really, really hard

00:13:58.781 --> 00:14:02.220
to be able to decipher, or to pinpoint,

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:03.463
the physics involved.

00:14:03.463 --> 00:14:05.290
We're adding extra dimensions,

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.120
we're adding extra particles,

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.650
we're adding all kinds
of extra crazy ideas,

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:12.070
and none of them have yet panned out

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.770
in a scientifically verifiable way.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:14.950
- And let me just say, do you think we're

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:16.330
happy about a Big Bang?

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:17.703
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:14:17.703 --> 00:14:18.536
(laughs)

00:14:18.536 --> 00:14:19.369
That's a great point.

00:14:19.369 --> 00:14:21.420
- This is like weird stuff, all right?

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.381
But evidence points to that.

00:14:24.381 --> 00:14:26.418
The universe we now occupy

00:14:26.418 --> 00:14:29.437
can be described by what happens

00:14:29.437 --> 00:14:33.443
if you had a Big Bang as accounted for

00:14:33.443 --> 00:14:37.250
with a small, dense, hot early beginning.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.860
And it gives us the amount of hydrogen,

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:41.672
and helium, and neutrinos, and

00:14:41.672 --> 00:14:45.520
the age, and the density, and the

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.018
distribution, all of
this comes out of that.

00:14:48.018 --> 00:14:51.087
If you have a better idea, fine.

00:14:51.087 --> 00:14:52.440
We'll take it.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.650
But until then, we're
stuck with the Big Bang.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.507
How's that, is that another
way to think about it?

00:14:57.507 --> 00:14:58.710
- I think that's a great point, yeah.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:02.832
The imperfections of a scientific
theory drive people nuts.

00:15:02.832 --> 00:15:05.480
We want theories to be perfect,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:07.674
but they're not, and that's where the

00:15:07.674 --> 00:15:09.730
science and the learning happens.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:11.938
- But what you don't know is whether

00:15:11.938 --> 00:15:13.530
the addition is just an add-on,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:14.910
or whether you have to
throw out everything

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:16.050
and come up with a new idea.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:18.735
- Do you think that last
gazillionth of a second

00:15:18.735 --> 00:15:22.810
will be explained, or
is it even explainable?

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.340
- We've got top people
working on it right now.

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.123
They're called string theorists.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:28.220
Top people.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.077
- And cosmologists in general, yeah.

00:15:30.077 --> 00:15:31.570
- [Neil] Top people.

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:32.967
- String theorists is just one group

00:15:32.967 --> 00:15:36.270
of promising paths,

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:38.376
but all of cosmology is really about

00:15:38.376 --> 00:15:40.623
trying to get that last little tiny bit.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:43.919
- Chris Carlton on Facebook asks,

00:15:43.919 --> 00:15:46.711
"Why aren't we expanding with the universe

00:15:46.711 --> 00:15:48.868
if we are part of the universe?"

00:15:48.868 --> 00:15:49.870
- Ah.

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:50.872
- Ooh.

00:15:50.872 --> 00:15:52.210
- I get asked that question
a lot by my students.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:53.470
- And I'm gonna add a coda to that,

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.226
will I ever break six foot?

00:15:55.226 --> 00:15:57.093
(laughs)

00:15:57.093 --> 00:15:58.380
- Oh, you're still growing?

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:00.750
- Well, I'm hopefully still expanding.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.265
- That can help you there.

00:16:02.265 --> 00:16:03.994
We will get to the answer
to that when we return

00:16:03.994 --> 00:16:08.820
for a second segment of Summer School.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:09.653
StarTalk.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:15.150
We're back in our second segment
of StarTalk Summer School.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:16.280
When you have to go to summer school,

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:18.120
you have to bring in the big guns.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:19.341
Charles Liu,

00:16:19.341 --> 00:16:20.174
(laughs)

00:16:20.174 --> 00:16:21.150
professor.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.010
- Thank you Neil.

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:23.280
- Professor Charles Liu.

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:24.591
CUNY Staten Island.

00:16:24.591 --> 00:16:25.820
Matt Kirshen,

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.002
- The little gun.

00:16:27.002 --> 00:16:28.990
(laughs)

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.208
The littlest gun.

00:16:30.208 --> 00:16:31.540
- Wait, so how tall are
you, how tall are you?

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:32.480
- Like five five.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.460
- Five five and 120 pounds.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:35.690
- Yep.

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:36.870
- Eight and a half stone.

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:37.703
Let's be specific.

00:16:37.703 --> 00:16:39.570
- Excuse me, eight and a half stone?

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.070
- Yeah, 'cause Britain really doesn't know

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.462
what units to use.

00:16:42.462 --> 00:16:43.550
(laughs)

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:45.920
We're metric sometimes, sometimes we're--

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.660
- Which stone, you know?

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:49.120
- In some cases we're ahead of you,

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:50.920
we do fruit and vegemetric,

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:52.980
we normally do Celsius for temperature,

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.172
but then we'll do stones--

00:16:54.172 --> 00:16:55.450
- Yeah, you have issues.

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:56.960
You have metric issues.

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.345
- We're comparing things to
the size of animals, it's--

00:16:59.345 --> 00:17:00.485
(laughs)

00:17:00.485 --> 00:17:02.130
- So that's where the stones come from.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:02.963
- I don't even know--

00:17:02.963 --> 00:17:04.494
- No, that's for the hands, you have hands

00:17:04.494 --> 00:17:05.658
for the height of horses.

00:17:05.658 --> 00:17:06.491
- We have hands and--

00:17:06.491 --> 00:17:07.550
- Whose hands?

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:09.010
Whose feet?

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:11.310
So, we left off at the first segment,

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:12.540
a question--

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.910
- About, yes, if the universe is expanding

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:15.960
and we're part of the universe,

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.839
why are we not expanding?

00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:21.263
- And the answer is
electromagnetic forces.

00:17:21.263 --> 00:17:25.270
See, the expansion of the
universe is its own thing.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:30.150
But it does not counteract
on small scales.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:33.270
Things like atoms and
molecules pulling one another,

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.120
or electrons and protons, or--

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:36.430
- Literally, the atoms and molecules

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.261
in his pinky that he's pulling.

00:17:38.261 --> 00:17:41.540
As he picks his fingers on his hands, yes.

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:42.842
- That's an excellent point, yeah,

00:17:42.842 --> 00:17:45.150
so the reason my pinky nail is not

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.910
expanding away from my pinky finger

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:49.660
is because they're being held together

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:51.331
by other kinds of forces.

00:17:51.331 --> 00:17:53.013
- Way stronger forces.

00:17:53.013 --> 00:17:54.390
- In the shorts.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:55.829
- In the short distance.

00:17:55.829 --> 00:17:56.800
- Yeah, in the short distances.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:58.700
So out at the distances of

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.730
millions or billions of light years,

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.800
the expansion of the universe dominates.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:04.954
Even galaxies are carried
along for the ride.

00:18:04.954 --> 00:18:09.027
But, on scales of humans,
or even planets, really,

00:18:09.027 --> 00:18:11.470
those other forces are much stronger.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:13.020
- Even the solar system is not expanding.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:14.786
- That, okay, 'cause I always,

00:18:14.786 --> 00:18:17.260
I've been given the analogy in the past of

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:18.520
blowing up the balloon and watching

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.430
the dots get further apart.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:21.263
- On the surface--

00:18:21.263 --> 00:18:22.682
- That's right.

00:18:22.682 --> 00:18:24.000
- But is it then almost more like sort of

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.681
blowing up a globe that has, like,

00:18:26.681 --> 00:18:29.650
ice sheets floating on it,

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:32.290
but the ice sheets stay in one clump--

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:34.307
- That is a correct,
better assessment to--

00:18:34.307 --> 00:18:36.334
- And they become further
apart as the globe expands.

00:18:36.334 --> 00:18:38.970
- Yeah, on a balloon, if
you drew a galaxy on it,

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:40.990
that galaxy would actually get bigger.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:42.539
But that's not what happens.

00:18:42.539 --> 00:18:44.054
- Put like little sticky
notes on the balloon

00:18:44.054 --> 00:18:45.055
and that would work.

00:18:45.055 --> 00:18:46.052
- Right, sticky notes that sort of

00:18:46.052 --> 00:18:47.056
have the ability to slide.

00:18:47.056 --> 00:18:48.100
- Lifesavers, you glue
'em on and they'll just

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.178
stay in one shape--

00:18:49.178 --> 00:18:50.011
- Yeah, you can do that, too.

00:18:50.011 --> 00:18:51.420
- So the sticky notes
themselves or the lifesavers

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:52.820
would become further apart,

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:54.990
but within themselves they
would stay the same size.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:55.823
- Correct.

00:18:55.823 --> 00:18:56.656
- You got it.

00:18:56.656 --> 00:18:57.817
- Okay.

00:18:57.817 --> 00:18:58.820
- [Neil] Yeah.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:18:59.822
- It's a very good question.

00:18:59.822 --> 00:19:00.824
- I understand something a lot better now.

00:19:00.824 --> 00:19:01.828
- We got good question
people out there, man.

00:19:01.828 --> 00:19:03.503
- What an excellent audience StarTalk has.

00:19:03.503 --> 00:19:05.070
- This is an excellent audience.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:06.656
Unless they just culled from--

00:19:06.656 --> 00:19:07.834
(laughs)

00:19:07.834 --> 00:19:08.930
- No, no, no, no.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:09.890
I'm sure these are--

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:12.117
- We'll check with our
researcher and find out.

00:19:12.117 --> 00:19:13.750
All right, Matt, next.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:17.333
- All right, from Ben Rattner
at Ben Makes TV on Twitter--

00:19:17.333 --> 00:19:18.880
- Oh, that guy.

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:20.122
- I've heard of that guy before.

00:19:20.122 --> 00:19:24.657
- Ben says, "Please describe
the laws of physics."

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:27.347
It sounds like we're doing Ben's homework.

00:19:27.347 --> 00:19:28.180
- Oh.
- Oh.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:29.013
- No, here's what I'm doing.

00:19:29.013 --> 00:19:30.040
I'm gonna pull rank here, you ready?

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:31.040
- Go ahead.
- Okay.

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:36.350
In one of my books I have a chapter called

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:38.713
On Earth as in the Heavens.

00:19:38.713 --> 00:19:39.783
- Ooh.

00:19:39.783 --> 00:19:41.810
- It's all about the effort to

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:43.582
discover laws of physics on Earth,

00:19:43.582 --> 00:19:46.420
and the question about whether they apply

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:48.190
in places other than Earth.

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.780
And it's not a given that
that should be the case.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:51.613
- [Charles] Okay, okay.

00:19:51.613 --> 00:19:53.840
- It might've been that
something you discover

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.040
here on Earth is different
on Mars, or different on--

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.000
but it turns out it's not, it's the same.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.430
- What I would be interested in is not

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:02.930
necessarily the laws of physics,

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:04.500
'cause that's what I
live with all my life.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:05.892
- Do you?

00:20:05.892 --> 00:20:06.730
- [Charles] Yes.

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:07.891
- Do you have a choice?

00:20:07.891 --> 00:20:09.252
- But.

00:20:09.252 --> 00:20:11.183
What are the laws of psychics?

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:13.803
- I don't understand.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:19.240
- Well, laws of physics,
right, natural stuff,

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.520
predictable, whatever.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.550
But the laws of psychics,

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:26.462
like people who make
predictions about the future,

00:20:26.462 --> 00:20:29.650
people who cast themselves as those who

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.018
understand things that are beyond nature,

00:20:32.018 --> 00:20:35.630
what are the laws of psychics?

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:36.946
- Maybe there are no laws.

00:20:36.946 --> 00:20:40.766
- Well then why is there a
major in psychic studies?

00:20:40.766 --> 00:20:43.080
- Not in any university I know of,

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:43.913
unless it's in Arizona.

00:20:43.913 --> 00:20:44.746
- Astrology.

00:20:45.598 --> 00:20:46.993
Yeah, I think it's in Arizona, yeah.

00:20:46.993 --> 00:20:47.860
- Yeah, other than Arizona,

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:49.918
I don't think any accredited university

00:20:49.918 --> 00:20:53.030
has a major in astrology or psychics.

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.996
- Yeah, but surely if
such a major does exist

00:20:55.996 --> 00:20:59.573
then there must be some
laws that they must follow.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:02.263
- Okay.

00:21:02.263 --> 00:21:03.230
I've never been curious about that.

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:04.480
Maybe I should be.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:05.896
- [Charles] I don't know.

00:21:05.896 --> 00:21:06.820
- Yeah.

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:10.298
Here's the thing, the
headline you've never seen,

00:21:10.298 --> 00:21:15.298
Psychic Predicts Winning the
Lottery Number a Second Time.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:16.627
(laughs)

00:21:16.627 --> 00:21:18.770
- That's true, that's true.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:19.603
- You don't get that.

00:21:19.603 --> 00:21:20.640
- That's true.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.493
Well, we'll have to ask this questioner

00:21:22.493 --> 00:21:25.864
yeah, what Ben Makes TV thinks about

00:21:25.864 --> 00:21:28.275
the laws of psychics.

00:21:28.275 --> 00:21:30.948
- But otherwise, the laws
of physics are very simple.

00:21:30.948 --> 00:21:33.630
I mean, they fit, you know,

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:35.950
I remember I was in school,

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:37.730
and somebody was taking
an accounting class,

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:39.430
and I saw the book that
they were carrying,

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.270
it had like a million pages in the book.

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:45.540
And my book, which was on like
all of gravitational physics,

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:47.260
had like a third that many pages.

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:49.270
So I can understand the whole universe

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:50.640
based on what's in my book,

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:52.130
but they need a book four times as fat

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:53.820
just to be able to do somebody's taxes.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.000
- Fair enough.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:56.648
- 'Cause you can't deduce
the tax code from--

00:21:56.648 --> 00:21:57.670
(laughs)

00:21:57.670 --> 00:21:59.340
- Thank you, that's the difference.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:00.430
The good thing about physics is,

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:03.130
you learn some rules and then
all the rest derive from them.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:03.963
- [Charles] Pretty much.

00:22:03.963 --> 00:22:05.470
- Very powerful.

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:07.787
- And then every so often
someone comes along goes,

00:22:07.787 --> 00:22:09.806
"Actually, those rules
are all slightly wrong."

00:22:09.806 --> 00:22:10.639
(laughs)

00:22:10.639 --> 00:22:11.806
- Yeah.

00:22:11.806 --> 00:22:14.227
- What we have, we have
Newton's F equals MA,

00:22:14.227 --> 00:22:15.250
you got E equals MC squared,

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:17.890
you got Maxwell's equations, we're done.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.136
- Practically.
- pretty much, yeah.

00:22:20.136 --> 00:22:21.456
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:21.456 --> 00:22:23.431
- Throw in a few quantum
physics equations--

00:22:23.431 --> 00:22:24.968
- Years worth of--

00:22:24.968 --> 00:22:26.248
- Schrodinger's equation.

00:22:26.248 --> 00:22:27.624
- Yeah, years worth of physics studied

00:22:27.624 --> 00:22:29.019
is just F on side, Force,

00:22:29.019 --> 00:22:32.950
and then MA, mass times
acceleration on the other side,

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:36.010
and you just change F in a
gazillion different ways,

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.290
you change MA in a
gazillion different ways,

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:41.757
and from that springs forth physics.

00:22:41.757 --> 00:22:42.995
- Unfolds for free.

00:22:42.995 --> 00:22:44.520
- Yeah, absolutely, absolutely.

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:45.787
- Yeah, it's beautiful.

00:22:45.787 --> 00:22:46.713
- Great question.

00:22:46.713 --> 00:22:48.750
- I really enjoy this question,

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:50.642
because apart from everything else

00:22:50.642 --> 00:22:51.480
it came from a four-year-old.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:52.848
- Ooh!

00:22:52.848 --> 00:22:54.076
- The four-year-old son--

00:22:54.076 --> 00:22:54.909
- You should tell us
after, when we're stumped.

00:22:54.909 --> 00:22:56.000
- Yeah, yeah yeah yeah.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.373
- The four-year-old son
of Pinty from Laos asks--

00:22:59.373 --> 00:23:00.920
- Laos, ooh.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:01.753
- Nice.

00:23:01.753 --> 00:23:05.417
- "Why do punctured balloons
fly around chaotically?

00:23:05.417 --> 00:23:07.985
"Why doesn't it fly on a straight line?"

00:23:07.985 --> 00:23:09.633
- Wonderful question.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.070
When you have a rocket, actually,

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:17.080
you know how they travel
in straight lines,

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.130
and we're always very impressed, right?

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.990
But what we don't see
is the amount of control

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:25.270
mechanisms and structures
within the rocket

00:23:25.270 --> 00:23:27.121
that make sure the exhaust comes out

00:23:27.121 --> 00:23:31.633
in a very orderly, and a very
directional fashion, right?

00:23:31.633 --> 00:23:35.011
When you puncture a balloon,
the exhaust that's coming out

00:23:35.011 --> 00:23:39.070
is coming out in a way
that's poorly controlled.

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:41.380
It's not a pin-pricked hole for example.

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.147
There's a difference, you can try this,

00:23:43.147 --> 00:23:46.300
you put a little tiny hole in a balloon,

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:48.160
as opposed to a large hole in the balloon.

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.780
The larger the hole, the
more chaotic the flow goes.

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:52.750
So it's a matter of whether
or not you can control

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:56.160
the air coming out in a
reasonable or linear way

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:57.400
compared with whether it's just

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:58.410
rushing out all at once.

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:00.667
- Well also, you'd want
the movement of the air

00:24:00.667 --> 00:24:05.222
to line up with the center
of mass of the balloon, okay?

00:24:05.222 --> 00:24:07.910
So if that lines up, then the balloon will

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:09.800
just get pushed in one direction.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.490
If the jet's air is coming out at an angle

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:15.250
different from straight
to the center of mass,

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:16.976
you'll start rotating the balloon.

00:24:16.976 --> 00:24:18.900
Plus, a balloon is not symmetric.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.420
The bottom of it where you got the knot--

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.170
- [Charles] The knot, yeah, the knot.

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:25.050
- So the balloon's
weight is not symmetric.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:26.260
- And the center of
the mass of the balloon

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:28.623
would also move around
as the balloon deflates.

00:24:28.623 --> 00:24:29.624
- Yes!

00:24:29.624 --> 00:24:30.680
Yes!

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.081
- That's right.

00:24:32.081 --> 00:24:33.230
- So, what you have to do
is configure something,

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:37.970
like get a straw, maybe, to
guide the air a little better,

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:39.960
have some stabilizers, and then you can

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:41.145
make a balloon rocket.

00:24:41.145 --> 00:24:43.160
- That would be a lot of fun.

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.510
- But I love that question.

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:45.809
- It's a great question.

00:24:45.809 --> 00:24:48.251
- That sounds like a kid that's
tearin' up birthday parties.

00:24:48.251 --> 00:24:49.254
(laughs)

00:24:49.254 --> 00:24:51.748
- I want that kid on my research staff.

00:24:51.748 --> 00:24:52.770
(laughs)

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.470
- By the way, most of the time
when you pin prick a balloon,

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:56.610
it blows up.

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:57.944
- [Matt] Right.

00:24:57.944 --> 00:24:59.301
- Right, so you have to do this carefully.

00:24:59.301 --> 00:25:00.270
- Yeah, I learned as a child,

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:02.800
if you put a piece of tape on the balloon,

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.130
then you poke the hole through the tape,

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:08.122
then the rubber, or the
outside, like, flexible stuff,

00:25:08.122 --> 00:25:10.918
doesn't rupture in a rip, and
you just get a little hole,

00:25:10.918 --> 00:25:12.731
then you can have it--

00:25:12.731 --> 00:25:13.967
- So you've done this before.

00:25:13.967 --> 00:25:15.220
- Of course, hasn't everybody?

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:16.877
- Okay, no, I haven't put tape
on a balloon to puncture it.

00:25:16.877 --> 00:25:18.160
- Ooh, give it a try sometime--

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.010
- But it's way more fun to just pop them.

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:21.930
- It's too fast.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:22.763
- It is too fast.

00:25:22.763 --> 00:25:24.194
- It's too fast.
- It's too fast.

00:25:24.194 --> 00:25:25.027
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:25:25.027 --> 00:25:26.033
- You are correct.

00:25:26.033 --> 00:25:26.866
(laughs)

00:25:26.866 --> 00:25:27.699
Okay, I will try this from now on.

00:25:27.699 --> 00:25:28.890
Any kind of, masking tape, or--

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:31.070
- I used, like, plastic invisible tape--

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:32.320
- Okay, Scotch tape.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:33.153
- Scotch tape, yeah.

00:25:33.153 --> 00:25:35.510
- Wait wait, now you put it
on both sides of the balloon--

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:36.972
- There you go.

00:25:36.972 --> 00:25:38.528
- And open it up, the balloon
won't know where to go.

00:25:38.528 --> 00:25:39.735
- It won't know where to
go, it'll start spinning.

00:25:39.735 --> 00:25:40.568
(laughs)

00:25:40.568 --> 00:25:41.930
That would be fun.

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:44.310
All right, four year
old balloon experiments.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.143
- I like that.

00:25:45.143 --> 00:25:47.267
So, Pulsa Priv on Instagram says,

00:25:47.267 --> 00:25:48.727
"Hi, quick question.

00:25:48.727 --> 00:25:50.597
"How would you explain neutrinos to

00:25:50.597 --> 00:25:53.360
"dumb teenagers who know
nothing about astrophysics?"

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:55.011
- Okay, okay, first--

00:25:55.011 --> 00:25:55.844
- And the question, it doesn't make clear

00:25:55.844 --> 00:25:57.816
whether they themselves
are the dumb teenager

00:25:57.816 --> 00:25:58.969
or are trying to--

00:25:58.969 --> 00:26:00.984
- It's a friend, a friend, I
want to explain to a friend.

00:26:00.984 --> 00:26:02.200
- Let me jump in right now and say that

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:04.077
there are no dumb teenagers.

00:26:04.077 --> 00:26:05.475
There just aren't.

00:26:05.475 --> 00:26:09.380
I'm an educator, and maybe
I'm showing my bias here,

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:11.810
but I've never found an
actual dumb teenager.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:13.480
They can pretend to be dumb,

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:15.000
they may think they're dumb--

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:16.003
- 'Cause it's cool.

00:26:16.003 --> 00:26:16.836
- Yeah, but they're not actually dumb.

00:26:16.836 --> 00:26:20.350
I want people always to
feel like they are smart,

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:22.094
because they are, and not have to act dumb

00:26:22.094 --> 00:26:24.610
or pretend to be dumb to be cool.

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:26.930
All right, so there's no such
thing as a dumb teenager,

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:29.420
that's just my opinion, sorry,
had to get it out there.

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:31.199
Now, to answer the question.

00:26:31.199 --> 00:26:34.179
A neutrino is just a little tiny particle

00:26:34.179 --> 00:26:39.179
that comes out so that
in atomic interactions--

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:41.390
- [Neil] Nuclear interactions.

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:46.232
- In nuclear interactions
energy is properly balanced.

00:26:46.232 --> 00:26:51.060
Both in the motion and in the amount.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:52.930
I think that's really what a simple way

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:54.605
of describing a neutrino is.

00:26:54.605 --> 00:26:55.610
- Yeah, it's a pretty weird particle.

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:57.229
'Cause no one knew they existed,

00:26:57.229 --> 00:26:59.610
and there was an imbalance
in the experiments

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.580
that were being done in nuclear physics,

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:03.328
and it was Enrico Fermi who said,

00:27:03.328 --> 00:27:06.117
"There's gotta be a particle carrying

00:27:06.117 --> 00:27:08.460
"away this momentum, there has to."

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:09.630
We look and we don't find--

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:10.480
Gotta keep looking.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:11.510
It's gotta be.

00:27:11.510 --> 00:27:12.510
What properties should it have?

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:14.420
Well it can't have any charge,

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.619
so it's gotta be neutral,

00:27:15.619 --> 00:27:19.250
and it's gotta be really
low mass and little.

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:21.331
So it's gotta be little and neutral.

00:27:21.331 --> 00:27:23.020
And--

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:24.040
- Well, the funny thing is, of course--

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:26.094
- So in Italian, neutrino.

00:27:26.094 --> 00:27:28.040
Like bambino, neutrino.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:30.560
- So it's just the diminutive
version of neutral.

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:32.050
- Of neutral, or of a neutron, right.

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:34.140
- But the funny thing is
when this was first proposed

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:36.400
the neutron had not yet been discovered.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:37.820
So when the neutron was discovered,

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:39.027
people are like, "Oh, is this it?"

00:27:39.027 --> 00:27:41.900
And did more calculations
said, "Nope, still not it."

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:43.780
There's something that's even
smaller that the neutron.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:45.560
- That has no charge, right, right right.

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:49.010
So yeah, it's necessary to
what's going on in the universe,

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.300
but it's very hard to stop,

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.420
it plows through anything.

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:55.220
What's the number of
neutrinos that go through

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:56.820
your thumb every second from the sun?

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:57.790
- Many trillions.

00:27:57.790 --> 00:27:58.623
- Trillions.

00:27:58.623 --> 00:27:59.637
- Many, many trillions.

00:27:59.637 --> 00:28:00.661
- Can you feel it?

00:28:00.661 --> 00:28:01.494
- Matt, can you feel it?
- Can't feel it.

00:28:01.494 --> 00:28:02.327
- I mean, I can a bit.

00:28:02.327 --> 00:28:03.160
(laughs)

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:04.380
It could be the air
conditioning, I don't know,

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:05.250
it's hard to tell.

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:06.670
So how do we--

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:07.890
- 'Cause they don't
interact and that's why

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:08.910
they were so hard to detect.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.290
- But they do interact
very minimally, right?

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:12.680
We can detect them.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:16.636
- And it was one of the great
experiments in astrophysics

00:28:16.636 --> 00:28:20.113
that allowed us to find
neutrinos coming out of the Sun.

00:28:20.113 --> 00:28:24.166
What happened was that
people took a very large vat

00:28:24.166 --> 00:28:28.199
of dry cleaning fluid, very pure.

00:28:28.199 --> 00:28:31.911
Put it down more than
mile below the surface

00:28:31.911 --> 00:28:35.423
in South Dakota, and surrounded it--

00:28:35.423 --> 00:28:38.280
- I think it was just a
pre-existing salt mine, wasn't it?

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:39.370
- It was a gold mine.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:40.588
- Oh, gold mine.

00:28:40.588 --> 00:28:41.645
- The Homestake Gold Mine.

00:28:41.645 --> 00:28:42.737
- Gold mine, not a salt mine, okay.

00:28:42.737 --> 00:28:44.490
- And it was sunk all the
way down to the bottom,

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:45.990
they put it there and they surrounded it '

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:47.370
with a lot of cameras.

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:49.649
And so, they just watched this tank

00:28:49.649 --> 00:28:53.280
of very pure cleaning fluid,

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:55.260
and when a neutrino hit,

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:57.780
even though trillions and trillions

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:58.860
pass through every second,

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:00.210
they might only get one neutrino

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.250
hit every long once in awhile.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.314
And when that happened, there
would be a flash of light.

00:29:04.314 --> 00:29:06.670
And so they'd watch it, and make sure that

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:08.110
the flash of light is not caused by

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:11.420
anything other than a solar neutrino.

00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:13.212
And that's what (mumbles).

00:29:13.212 --> 00:29:14.215
- Why cleaning fluid?

00:29:14.215 --> 00:29:17.533
- Turns out that that particular molecule,

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:19.470
perchlorate--

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:21.580
- Yeah, I think it needed
the chlorine in it.

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:24.198
- Yeah, had a special, nice property

00:29:24.198 --> 00:29:26.850
that it would, when hit by a neutrino,

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:29.600
with that interaction, very
rare, occasional interaction

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:30.950
would cause a flash of light.

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:32.770
- How long ago did that experiment happen?

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:33.724
Was it recent?

00:29:33.724 --> 00:29:34.736
- Couple of decades, yeah.

00:29:34.736 --> 00:29:35.751
- Several decades ago.

00:29:35.751 --> 00:29:36.610
- 70s, 80s.

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:39.050
- Ray Davis, Jr. was the experimentalist,

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:40.437
John Bacall was the theorist that was--

00:29:40.437 --> 00:29:42.237
- And Ray Davis got the Nobel
Prize, I think, for that,

00:29:42.237 --> 00:29:43.410
but not John Bacall.

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:44.850
- Yes, yes.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:46.370
- People didn't fully understand that.

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:47.890
You have the theory and the experiment,

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:49.660
and the observations, they all have to

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:52.093
come together to make that discovery.

00:29:52.093 --> 00:29:53.148
- That's correct.

00:29:53.148 --> 00:29:55.380
But that was a real triumph.

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:57.970
And it led to a secondary
discovery, actually,

00:29:57.970 --> 00:29:59.320
because it turned out that the

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:02.210
number of neutrinos that were
being detected from the Sun

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:05.260
were fewer than we expected.

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:06.477
And so for a moment, people thought,

00:30:06.477 --> 00:30:08.067
"Wait a second, is the Sun dead and

00:30:08.067 --> 00:30:09.625
"we just don't know it yet?"

00:30:09.625 --> 00:30:12.020
It was known as the
Solar Neutrino Problem.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:14.870
Because those neutrinos had to be produced

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:17.030
in order for nuclear
fusion to be going on.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:19.500
So if they were half
as many as we expected,

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:22.044
then maybe the Sun itself was starting to

00:30:22.044 --> 00:30:23.740
run out of fuel.

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:24.573
- Scary prospect.

00:30:24.573 --> 00:30:26.062
- Yes, scary prospect.

00:30:26.062 --> 00:30:28.060
Turned out, though, that it
was just something happening

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:31.270
in our upper atmosphere,
called neutrino oscillations.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:32.993
Another amazing discovery.

00:30:34.266 --> 00:30:36.760
- Yeah, so it'd be I
throw you a basketball,

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:39.110
but you catch a football.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:39.943
- Right.

00:30:39.943 --> 00:30:42.225
- So the experiment was
designed to detect basketballs,

00:30:42.225 --> 00:30:46.190
and what you were actually
receiving footballs.

00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:47.820
- And what it is is someone in between us

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:48.653
is swapping them out.

00:30:48.653 --> 00:30:49.944
- That's right.

00:30:49.944 --> 00:30:50.942
(laughs)

00:30:50.942 --> 00:30:51.775
And it was amazing, that
was what was happening.

00:30:51.775 --> 00:30:53.860
These neutrinos come in
different flavors, it turns out.

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:54.693
Who knew?

00:30:54.693 --> 00:30:55.526
- Who knew at the time?

00:30:55.526 --> 00:30:57.850
- Oh okay, so there isn't
just one neutrino particle,

00:30:57.850 --> 00:30:59.310
there's three.

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:00.143
- Yeah.
- Okay.

00:31:00.143 --> 00:31:01.672
- Three different kinds.

00:31:01.672 --> 00:31:03.803
Electron neutrinos, Tao
neutrinos, and Mu neutrinos.

00:31:03.803 --> 00:31:06.542
- And their antimatter counterparts.

00:31:06.542 --> 00:31:07.870
- And the antimatter counterparts.

00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:08.903
- There are really six.

00:31:08.903 --> 00:31:09.736
- Yeah.

00:31:09.736 --> 00:31:10.569
- Yeah, it's pretty cool.

00:31:10.569 --> 00:31:11.402
- Physics is amazing, isn't it?

00:31:11.402 --> 00:31:12.290
I love summer school.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:13.220
Great question.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:14.230
- Summer school, next question.

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:17.157
- All right, Kyle Ryan
Toth on Patreon asks,

00:31:17.157 --> 00:31:19.567
"Ignoring the cold, could
a settlement survive

00:31:19.567 --> 00:31:21.227
"on the surface of Pluto?

00:31:21.227 --> 00:31:23.327
"What would radiation levels be like,

00:31:23.327 --> 00:31:25.095
"are their any useful resources

00:31:25.095 --> 00:31:26.860
"other than water-ice?"

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:27.961
- Hold me back.

00:31:27.961 --> 00:31:29.060
(laughs)

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:32.403
We'll get to that question
when we return on StarTalk.

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:37.490
Matt, before the break
you had a Pluto question.

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.601
- It is, yeah--

00:31:38.601 --> 00:31:40.153
- You know me and Pluto have history.

00:31:40.153 --> 00:31:40.986
(laughs)

00:31:40.986 --> 00:31:42.560
But we buried the hatchet long ago.

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:44.060
- Well, whether it's a planet or not,

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.100
could a settlement survive on it?

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:47.250
- Oh.

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.230
Well you can ask why would
you want to do such a thing?

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:51.063
- [Matt] Right.

00:31:51.063 --> 00:31:53.430
- Because, just take for example, Charles,

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:57.660
is there a line of people
waiting to settle in Antarctica?

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:58.493
- No.

00:31:58.493 --> 00:32:00.921
- And Antarctica is warmer and is

00:32:00.921 --> 00:32:04.270
balmier and wetter than Pluto.

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:05.492
- And there's penguins.

00:32:05.492 --> 00:32:06.325
(laughs)

00:32:06.325 --> 00:32:07.777
To the best of my knowledge,
there are none on Pluto.

00:32:07.777 --> 00:32:10.216
- And in a pinch, we can
eat a penguin, right?

00:32:10.216 --> 00:32:11.049
- That's right.

00:32:11.049 --> 00:32:13.007
- So Pluto, in principle,

00:32:13.007 --> 00:32:14.880
we could just pitch a tent anywhere

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:15.830
and just bring enough resources, right?

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:16.910
- Yeah, yeah.

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:18.500
This settlement could survive there,

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:20.367
as long as you could shield yourself from

00:32:20.367 --> 00:32:23.170
the cosmic rays that hit it,

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:25.320
and as long as you can keep yourself warm,

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:28.390
because the temperature's
so low out at that distance.

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:30.400
- And food, you need a
way to generate food.

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:31.479
- Yeah, you need food.

00:32:31.479 --> 00:32:32.567
- How faint is the Sun,

00:32:32.567 --> 00:32:33.600
if I was standing on the surface of Pluto

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:34.790
and staring straight at the Sun,

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:36.740
which I presume I would be safe to do--

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:42.006
- The flux is only about
one 1600th the amount of

00:32:42.006 --> 00:32:44.450
flux that we get from
the Sun here on Earth.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:46.030
- Is it only that much?

00:32:46.030 --> 00:32:48.550
I thought, I'm going on memory now,

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:49.783
not on a calculation,

00:32:49.783 --> 00:32:53.524
that the Sun from Pluto is about

00:32:53.524 --> 00:32:55.671
like a full moon night here.

00:32:55.671 --> 00:32:57.303
Is it brighter than that?

00:32:57.303 --> 00:32:58.860
- Ooh, let me do that
quick calculation there.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.456
Well, absolute magnitude
negative 26 for the Sun,

00:33:01.456 --> 00:33:02.650
minus 15--

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:04.130
- No, 12, moon is 12.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:05.280
- So that's 14 magnitudes.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:06.113
- 14.

00:33:06.113 --> 00:33:07.660
- Soo that's a factor of--

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:08.540
- So seven--

00:33:08.540 --> 00:33:09.850
- Yeah.

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:12.187
- So seven, so 10 to the 14.

00:33:12.187 --> 00:33:13.700
- No, not quite that much.

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:15.301
- Yeah, seven magnitudes.

00:33:15.301 --> 00:33:17.062
Oh no, I'm sorry, five, five.

00:33:17.062 --> 00:33:20.137
So it's a factor of 100,000.

00:33:21.504 --> 00:33:22.619
- Yeah, right.

00:33:22.619 --> 00:33:23.577
So it's a little bit--

00:33:23.577 --> 00:33:24.902
- [Neil] And you said it's--

00:33:24.902 --> 00:33:28.441
- I said 16,000 because, so 40 AU, right?

00:33:28.441 --> 00:33:30.456
- Oh, you just divide it out, yeah, okay.

00:33:30.456 --> 00:33:33.345
(man speaks inaudibly off-screen).

00:33:33.345 --> 00:33:34.786
1600, yeah.

00:33:34.786 --> 00:33:35.791
- So, they're--

00:33:35.791 --> 00:33:37.179
- You think we need HuffPo
to find out if we're--

00:33:37.179 --> 00:33:38.182
(laughs)

00:33:38.182 --> 00:33:41.850
You're in the middle of an
active calculation here.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.210
You pull up a HuffPo page?

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:45.410
- No, no, this actually
brings up a very good point,

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:46.920
and I teach this to all my students.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:48.370
And since this is a summer school episode,

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:49.968
it's quite appropriate.

00:33:49.968 --> 00:33:51.770
- We are 40, Pluto is
40 times farther away

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:53.910
from the Sun than Earth is, on average,

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:55.070
and so it's one over that squared--

00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:56.536
- That's right.

00:33:56.536 --> 00:33:57.540
So that's where you get the 1600.

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:58.373
- One 1600th as bright.

00:33:58.373 --> 00:33:59.206
- That's right.

00:33:59.206 --> 00:34:00.810
But the idea that a person could--

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:01.920
- Inverse square law of light.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:02.753
Very cool.

00:34:02.753 --> 00:34:04.200
- The idea that a person can just

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:05.860
pull up the answer on Google faster than

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:07.100
we can do the calculation,

00:34:07.100 --> 00:34:09.670
brings up a really important
point about school in general,

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:11.430
and education in specific.

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.300
You might agree with this, Neil.

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:16.280
We can no longer think
that we are educated,

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:18.180
if all we can do is memorize facts

00:34:18.180 --> 00:34:21.020
or calculate things that
can already be calculated

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:22.230
and sit on a database.

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:24.591
We have the world's
information at our fingertips.

00:34:24.591 --> 00:34:27.472
The only way that we can remain viable as

00:34:27.472 --> 00:34:30.270
a productive member of society,

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:34.651
or as a civilization, is if
we are better than Google.

00:34:34.651 --> 00:34:36.330
We have to do that.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:37.930
And I'm just not picking
Google specifically--

00:34:37.930 --> 00:34:39.663
- Sounds like he doesn't wanna
be replaced with a robot.

00:34:39.663 --> 00:34:41.230
Matt, does that sound like that to you?

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:42.410
- It is absolutely right.

00:34:42.410 --> 00:34:45.650
We all can easily be
replaced by a search engine.

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:48.038
Our education system, our learning,

00:34:48.038 --> 00:34:51.737
our interaction with
nature and with the world

00:34:51.737 --> 00:34:55.344
and with other people must be
better than a search engine.

00:34:55.344 --> 00:34:57.263
- Charles for president,
Charles for president!

00:34:57.263 --> 00:34:58.288
(laughs)

00:34:58.288 --> 00:35:00.176
- We know that Charles is a
better singer than Google, so--

00:35:00.176 --> 00:35:01.179
(laughs)

00:35:01.179 --> 00:35:03.880
- I think that was Charles's
stump speech right there.

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:04.713
- Oh dear.

00:35:04.713 --> 00:35:07.248
- Well we are talking
about singing and the like,

00:35:07.248 --> 00:35:10.340
Marcus, and I'm gonna apologize
for butchering this name,

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:11.980
but Goyamarez?

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:13.980
I apologize if that is way off.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:17.157
On Patreon asks, "I had a
debate with a friend of mine,

00:35:17.157 --> 00:35:20.080
"where he said that scientists
hate arts in general.

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:21.097
- [Neil] (gasps)

00:35:21.097 --> 00:35:23.270
"I think that's not true," says Marcus.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:24.516
- Aaah!

00:35:24.516 --> 00:35:26.113
Excuse me while I go throw up.

00:35:26.113 --> 00:35:27.419
- "Dr. Tyson loves art, and he loves

00:35:27.419 --> 00:35:29.029
"the Starry Night by Van Gogh.

00:35:29.029 --> 00:35:30.927
"Could you please tell us
something about the subject?"

00:35:30.927 --> 00:35:31.969
- (coughs)

00:35:31.969 --> 00:35:32.802
- Charles, are you okay there?

00:35:32.802 --> 00:35:34.587
You seem put out somehow.

00:35:34.587 --> 00:35:36.128
- Science hates art?

00:35:36.128 --> 00:35:37.240
(retches)

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:38.130
No way.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:39.340
- Yeah, I don't know,

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:42.300
first, I don't know any
scientist who hates art, A.

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:45.520
B, many scientists I know
not only just don't hate it,

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:46.530
but love it.

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:51.530
C, one of the books behind you on a shelf

00:35:51.696 --> 00:35:53.744
is called Mathematics and Art,

00:35:53.744 --> 00:35:56.752
a book written by an art curator,

00:35:56.752 --> 00:35:59.750
who is fascinated by the role of science

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:01.370
as it has influenced art,

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:03.810
and I was privileged to be asked to

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:06.125
write the forward to that book.

00:36:06.125 --> 00:36:10.381
And, as the writer knows, I'm
a big fan of the Starry Night,

00:36:10.381 --> 00:36:12.816
by Vincent Van Gogh, 1889.

00:36:12.816 --> 00:36:16.955
And so I, and I'm not
unusual in this regard.

00:36:16.955 --> 00:36:20.644
Our colleagues love music, love art,

00:36:20.644 --> 00:36:24.320
on levels that you might
not even know or suspect,

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:25.780
because generally if they're in the news,

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:26.820
it's not because of that,

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:28.700
it's because of the science they're doing.

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:30.040
Oh no, no.

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:31.991
We're art-loving community.

00:36:31.991 --> 00:36:33.210
All the way back.

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:35.730
And why do you think universities call

00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:38.020
the schools of Arts and Sciences?

00:36:38.020 --> 00:36:39.320
We go way back.

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:40.963
- Yeah, way back.

00:36:40.963 --> 00:36:45.963
- To sides of a coin, each the pinnacle of

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:49.584
human creativity and expression.

00:36:49.584 --> 00:36:52.272
One constrained by the universe,

00:36:52.272 --> 00:36:56.693
the other constrained
by imagination itself.

00:36:57.980 --> 00:36:59.260
Science and art.

00:36:59.260 --> 00:37:00.590
- I could not say it better, Neil.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:01.760
It is absolutely true.

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:05.490
Science and art are
inextricably intertwined.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:08.673
There is no scientist I
know that doesn't like art.

00:37:08.673 --> 00:37:11.462
- Have him take up that
question with Leonardo da Vinci.

00:37:11.462 --> 00:37:12.464
(laughs)

00:37:12.464 --> 00:37:13.540
- Absolutely.

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:15.674
And Albert Einstein himself wrote in 1930

00:37:15.674 --> 00:37:18.970
that the sense of the mysterious,

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:22.480
the wish to be awed by
things we don't know

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:25.740
is the root of all great art and science.

00:37:25.740 --> 00:37:27.340
And I agree with that statement.

00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:31.910
- Trina Jennings on Facebook asks,

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:34.387
"If we can figure out the center of

00:37:34.387 --> 00:37:37.302
"the Milky Way galaxy
smells like raspberries,

00:37:37.302 --> 00:37:38.135
(laughs)

00:37:38.135 --> 00:37:40.777
"can we figure out what things
would smell like elsewhere?

00:37:40.777 --> 00:37:43.963
"Would the center of all
galaxies smell like raspberries?"

00:37:43.963 --> 00:37:44.966
- Okay, who said the center of the

00:37:44.966 --> 00:37:46.083
galaxy smelled like raspberries?

00:37:46.083 --> 00:37:48.016
- Oh, it's a cute press release about

00:37:48.016 --> 00:37:51.835
people who are looking at
molecules in gas clouds,

00:37:51.835 --> 00:37:53.657
and it turned out that they detected

00:37:53.657 --> 00:37:55.714
certain aromatic compounds,

00:37:55.714 --> 00:37:58.470
which are found in raspberries.

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:00.998
So they said, "Oh, it
smells like raspberries,

00:38:00.998 --> 00:38:03.931
"Oh, it smells like raspberries because

00:38:03.931 --> 00:38:07.398
"there are these volatile
organic compounds

00:38:07.398 --> 00:38:10.160
"that are in gas clouds near
the Milky Way galaxy center."

00:38:10.160 --> 00:38:11.440
- There's also a lot
of ethanol there, too,

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:13.014
you sound like a drunkard.

00:38:13.014 --> 00:38:14.959
- Yeah, it would smell
like a brewery, yeah.

00:38:14.959 --> 00:38:16.830
- But that'd be the yeast,

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:18.790
I think the yeast is the
predominant smell in a brewery.

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:19.814
- That's right.

00:38:19.814 --> 00:38:20.647
- A distillery.

00:38:20.647 --> 00:38:21.851
- A distillery, yeah.

00:38:21.851 --> 00:38:23.801
- There's a lot of organic
molecules in space, so--

00:38:23.801 --> 00:38:27.289
- And there's even sunless
tanning lotion, too.

00:38:27.289 --> 00:38:30.410
DHA in molecules over there, so you can

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:32.060
not only get a great suntan--

00:38:32.060 --> 00:38:33.100
- I've never looked, I--

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:33.933
- You can also smell--

00:38:33.933 --> 00:38:35.152
- I didn't know such a thing existed.

00:38:35.152 --> 00:38:36.153
- Yeah, yeah, sorry.

00:38:36.153 --> 00:38:37.680
- Up in the hood, it was not a--

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:38.683
(laughs)

00:38:38.683 --> 00:38:40.239
- You guys weren't tanning much?

00:38:40.239 --> 00:38:42.395
- Yeah, the CVS doesn't have
that on the front counter.

00:38:42.395 --> 00:38:43.228
- You don't do the spray tan?

00:38:43.228 --> 00:38:45.414
- No, no, no, no, not in the hood.

00:38:45.414 --> 00:38:49.500
So, it's a little deceptive to say

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.100
that space smells like that,

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:52.660
space smells like nothing.

00:38:52.660 --> 00:38:55.540
Unless you put molecules
where you're sniffing,

00:38:55.540 --> 00:38:57.323
and there are molecules
everywhere, and so there it is.

00:38:57.323 --> 00:39:00.070
Then you're smelling molecules in space,

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:02.273
rather than space itself.

00:39:02.273 --> 00:39:03.710
- Right, so to answer that
question constructively,

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:05.620
the answer is yes, as long as we can find

00:39:05.620 --> 00:39:08.208
the molecules that create
certain smells in our brains

00:39:08.208 --> 00:39:11.120
through our noses, in a location,

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:12.097
we can tell you exactly what that--

00:39:12.097 --> 00:39:13.190
- [Neil] What that would smell like.

00:39:13.190 --> 00:39:14.853
Precisely.

00:39:14.853 --> 00:39:16.550
- But there are a lot
of particles out there.

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:18.512
And they can smell like a
lot of different things.

00:39:18.512 --> 00:39:19.810
- [Neil] Indeed.

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:20.643
- Nice.

00:39:20.643 --> 00:39:22.156
Who wants a relativity question?

00:39:22.156 --> 00:39:23.010
- Bring it on.

00:39:23.010 --> 00:39:25.687
- Hussein Sagwani on Twitter says,

00:39:25.687 --> 00:39:27.767
"I'm still having a
hard time understanding

00:39:27.767 --> 00:39:29.607
"the concept of how if
your twin is on Earth

00:39:29.607 --> 00:39:31.547
"and you travel at almost
the speed of light,

00:39:31.547 --> 00:39:33.017
"you will not age as much as them

00:39:33.017 --> 00:39:34.337
"or something like that.

00:39:34.337 --> 00:39:36.137
"Can you really try to dumb it down,

00:39:36.137 --> 00:39:37.630
"or using analogies?"

00:39:37.630 --> 00:39:40.240
- Again, before Neil says anything--

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:41.660
- No, no I'm not saying anything.

00:39:41.660 --> 00:39:42.520
I'm out of this one, go.

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:45.010
- Please don't use the term "dumb down."

00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.650
We are not dumbing things down,

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.850
we are merely translating the concept

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:50.263
into a language that
everyone can understand.

00:39:50.263 --> 00:39:52.624
- In that case, can you smart it up?

00:39:52.624 --> 00:39:53.689
(laughs)

00:39:53.689 --> 00:39:55.340
- That's right, thank you Matt.

00:39:55.340 --> 00:39:57.008
Okay, here's the basic point.

00:39:57.008 --> 00:39:59.193
- [Neil] Smart it down, smart it down.

00:39:59.193 --> 00:40:02.647
- Time is experienced at different rates

00:40:02.647 --> 00:40:06.780
for people who are traveling
at different rates of speed.

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:09.926
That's a very, very complicated concept,

00:40:09.926 --> 00:40:13.700
if you are trying to lock it into our idea

00:40:13.700 --> 00:40:16.570
in our regular time that a
second is a second is a second.

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:19.090
But the moment you
acknowledge the possibility

00:40:19.090 --> 00:40:23.320
that time is a dimension like
length, width, and height,

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:25.744
and you can move through
it at different speeds,

00:40:25.744 --> 00:40:28.500
the the twin paradox
that is described here,

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:31.000
or other paradoxes
become not too difficult.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:31.833
'Cause what you're doing is

00:40:31.833 --> 00:40:33.970
measuring time in intervals, right?

00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:34.970
You're not measuring actually the

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:37.380
amount of time at this very instant,

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:38.779
but you're measuring the time from the

00:40:38.779 --> 00:40:41.930
time you experience one minute ago,

00:40:41.930 --> 00:40:44.380
to the time you experience
a minute from now.

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:47.010
That person who is traveling
at a different rate

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:49.300
through time and space will simply

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:51.929
experience a different interval.

00:40:51.929 --> 00:40:53.980
- And you'll both call it a minute.

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:54.813
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:40:54.813 --> 00:40:56.640
- But relative to each other,
they're different lengths.

00:40:56.640 --> 00:40:57.507
So, yeah.

00:40:57.507 --> 00:40:59.750
- And you only realize that once you

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:00.856
come back into contact with each other.

00:41:00.856 --> 00:41:01.689
- Correct, yeah, that's
right, that's right.

00:41:01.689 --> 00:41:06.416
And the way to know who will be younger,

00:41:06.416 --> 00:41:08.880
because both of them in motion will say

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:10.420
that the other one is,

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:12.580
their opposite clock is ticking slower,

00:41:12.580 --> 00:41:15.440
is that the, the twin who went out

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:18.896
had to slow down, turn
around, and come back.

00:41:18.896 --> 00:41:21.910
And that breaks the symmetry.

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:24.590
It's called a paradox,
because if I see you traveling

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:26.022
and your time is ticking more slowly,

00:41:26.022 --> 00:41:27.650
and motion is relative,

00:41:27.650 --> 00:41:30.470
and you see me, I'm
actually standing still,

00:41:30.470 --> 00:41:31.730
but as far as you're concerned

00:41:31.730 --> 00:41:33.030
you're standing still and I'm moving,

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:35.180
you see my time ticking slowly,

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:38.310
how is it that at the end of this exercise

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:39.970
one person is younger than the other?

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:41.988
- Right, how do we not
know, like, if, yeah,

00:41:41.988 --> 00:41:43.860
the train is going past the platform

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.584
rather than the platform
is going past the train.

00:41:45.584 --> 00:41:46.950
- That's one of the great
jokes against Einstein,

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:48.767
who said, "Hey Einstein, when
does Grand Central Station

00:41:48.767 --> 00:41:50.107
"arrive at the next train?"

00:41:50.107 --> 00:41:51.109
(laughs)

00:41:51.109 --> 00:41:53.734
Everyone was trying to
get their head around this

00:41:53.734 --> 00:41:55.010
back at the time.

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:56.816
- So it's specifically
the acceleration of the

00:41:56.816 --> 00:41:58.982
one twin who goes out on the space ship

00:41:58.982 --> 00:42:01.456
and goes out and then turns
around and comes back.

00:42:01.456 --> 00:42:02.459
It's that--

00:42:02.459 --> 00:42:03.462
- [Neil] That breaks the symmetry.

00:42:03.462 --> 00:42:04.295
- It's that acceleration
and then deceleration that--

00:42:04.295 --> 00:42:05.850
- Yeah, the whole thing is either

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:07.890
positive or negative acceleration,

00:42:07.890 --> 00:42:09.470
but it's just, it wouldn't matter,

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:10.303
it's just an acceleration.

00:42:10.303 --> 00:42:12.570
- The one that leaves and comes back,

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:16.440
that's the one that is the thing that

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:18.010
appears to be wrong.

00:42:18.010 --> 00:42:21.124
- So it's an uncomfortable concept

00:42:21.124 --> 00:42:23.370
because we don't experience
that in everyday life.

00:42:23.370 --> 00:42:24.688
- [Charles] That's right.

00:42:24.688 --> 00:42:25.521
- As active senses do,

00:42:25.521 --> 00:42:27.398
it was not necessary to know this

00:42:27.398 --> 00:42:29.488
on the plains of the Serengeti.

00:42:29.488 --> 00:42:32.260
To avoid getting eaten by a lion,

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:33.640
you didn't need to know relativity.

00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:36.380
- But it's absolutely
necessary in this day and age

00:42:36.380 --> 00:42:40.467
of atomic energy and
very very high speeds.

00:42:40.467 --> 00:42:44.710
- Matt, it's time for Lightning Round.

00:42:44.710 --> 00:42:45.543
- Oh yeah.

00:42:45.543 --> 00:42:46.376
- Okay.
- Nice.

00:42:46.376 --> 00:42:48.680
- See if the bell works,
(bell dings) it does.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:51.470
So Charles and I will try
to answer in sound bytes.

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:52.940
- All right, well we're gonna jump back

00:42:52.940 --> 00:42:54.010
from Einstein to Newton.

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:55.692
DJ Milky on Instagram says,

00:42:55.692 --> 00:42:57.847
"I read a few years back that the Earth is

00:42:57.847 --> 00:42:59.467
"technically falling into the Sun,

00:42:59.467 --> 00:43:01.087
"but doesn't actually go into the Sun.

00:43:01.087 --> 00:43:02.300
"Is that true?"

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.570
- Yes, and the reason it
doesn't go in is because

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:07.400
it's traveling at orbital
velocity around the Sun.

00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:09.327
- Yeah, if we traveled
any slower sideways,

00:43:09.327 --> 00:43:12.810
we would fall towards,
get closer to the Sun.

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:15.040
So we are falling towards the Sun,

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:17.369
but we are being held up, if you will,

00:43:17.369 --> 00:43:19.640
by our very high sideways speed.

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:20.760
So yeah, we got this.

00:43:20.760 --> 00:43:23.655
And Isaac Newton first demonstrated this--

00:43:23.655 --> 00:43:24.550
- [Charles] In Principia.

00:43:24.550 --> 00:43:25.550
- In Principia.

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:29.060
And first drew it in
The System of the World.

00:43:29.060 --> 00:43:32.390
That was his Cliff
Notes for his Principia.

00:43:32.390 --> 00:43:33.223
- [Charles] Terrific.

00:43:33.223 --> 00:43:34.591
- Written in English, very cool.

00:43:34.591 --> 00:43:35.683
All right (bell dings) you got it.

00:43:35.683 --> 00:43:39.148
- All right, Code Monkey
IA on Instagram says,

00:43:39.148 --> 00:43:42.477
"What causes the Earth's
magnetic poles to move,

00:43:42.477 --> 00:43:46.040
"and what would cause the
magnetic North and South to flip?"

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:47.393
- Ah, very good question.

00:43:47.393 --> 00:43:51.590
Our magnetic field is
created by the dynamic motion

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:54.685
of ferro-magnetic
materials inside our Earth.

00:43:54.685 --> 00:43:55.590
- Iron.

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:57.438
- [Charles] Yeah, almost all iron.

00:43:57.438 --> 00:43:58.836
- (laughs) It's almost entirely iron.

00:43:58.836 --> 00:43:59.838
- Almost all iron.

00:43:59.838 --> 00:44:01.330
And because of that, because
it's fluid and it moves,

00:44:01.330 --> 00:44:02.700
that's why our poles move.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:04.628
It's as if you had a
dancing magnet inside,

00:44:04.628 --> 00:44:08.330
but it's kind of somewhat
semi-solid, somewhat liquid.

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:09.740
- And it rotates, but not exactly the

00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:11.100
same rate that Earth does,

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:13.671
so all these dynamics
influence which direction

00:44:13.671 --> 00:44:15.941
on Earth's surface you find
the North and South Pole,

00:44:15.941 --> 00:44:18.364
and whether the North is up or down,

00:44:18.364 --> 00:44:21.040
relative to it, and history has shown

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:23.900
that the poles have flipped
multiple times in the past.

00:44:23.900 --> 00:44:25.961
Yeah, next (bell dings), oh, by the way,

00:44:25.961 --> 00:44:28.420
if our Earth cools completely,
and the core becomes solid,

00:44:28.420 --> 00:44:30.580
(snaps) the magnet field shuts off.

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.170
- It's frozen, yeah, yeah, it's done.

00:44:32.170 --> 00:44:33.400
- Good, (bell dings) good.

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:34.233
- All right--

00:44:34.233 --> 00:44:35.643
- Sorry, it wouldn't shut
off the magnetic field,

00:44:35.643 --> 00:44:36.920
it just changes the movement
of the magnetic field.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:38.161
- [Charles] Right.

00:44:38.161 --> 00:44:38.994
- Good, (bell dings) good, okay.

00:44:38.994 --> 00:44:40.137
- Evan Harrington on Facebook says,

00:44:40.137 --> 00:44:41.517
"If the universe is infinite,

00:44:41.517 --> 00:44:43.077
"then how could time exist other

00:44:43.077 --> 00:44:44.817
"than the meaning we give it?"

00:44:44.817 --> 00:44:48.884
- If the universe is
infinite is the key point.

00:44:48.884 --> 00:44:51.670
We don't know necessarily yet
that the universe is infinite.

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:52.890
- He's saying if it's infinite,

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:55.484
let's change the question.

00:44:55.484 --> 00:44:58.780
Suppose the universe had no
beginning and no known end,

00:44:58.780 --> 00:45:00.242
is infinite in time and space,

00:45:00.242 --> 00:45:02.460
what does it even mean to have a calendar?

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:03.945
- [Charles] Hmm, great point.

00:45:03.945 --> 00:45:04.947
- What are you measuring?

00:45:04.947 --> 00:45:06.710
Is that kinda the
paraphrase of that question?

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:07.543
- I think so, yes.
- I think so, yeah.

00:45:07.543 --> 00:45:08.376
- That's a good way to put it.

00:45:08.376 --> 00:45:09.209
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:09.209 --> 00:45:10.768
- And what we would be
measuring in time is

00:45:10.768 --> 00:45:14.524
the various atomic processes
going on in our bodies,

00:45:14.524 --> 00:45:17.390
and a clock, like the
definition of a second,

00:45:17.390 --> 00:45:20.010
is just a way to macroscopically allow us

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:21.780
to know whether we're getting older

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:22.613
or whether we're not.

00:45:22.613 --> 00:45:24.653
- So you need vibrating,
repeating phenomenon

00:45:24.653 --> 00:45:26.920
to measure time accurately at all.

00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:29.052
So in a universe where nothing repeats,

00:45:29.052 --> 00:45:33.920
there can be no measurement of time.

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:34.753
- Well--

00:45:34.753 --> 00:45:35.586
- What do you think of that?

00:45:35.586 --> 00:45:38.840
- If you can measure the passage of time,

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:40.080
or define the passage of time as

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:41.710
the expansion of the universe,

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:43.350
then you don't have to have a vibration,

00:45:43.350 --> 00:45:44.760
you can just have the change that's

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:47.570
going in a single direction
from smaller to larger.

00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:50.030
- Okay, so time, then,
gets measured by size.

00:45:50.030 --> 00:45:50.863
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:45:50.863 --> 00:45:51.800
- Size of things, interesting.

00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:53.110
- [Charles] Rather than repetition.

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:54.436
- Matt, last question, go for it.

00:45:54.436 --> 00:45:55.437
(bell dings)

00:45:55.437 --> 00:45:56.581
- All right, I'm gonna
go with this one, then.

00:45:56.581 --> 00:45:59.365
Billy from Queens here
out of Hunter College.

00:45:59.365 --> 00:46:00.198
- Give me some Billy.

00:46:00.198 --> 00:46:02.700
- Hunter College, City
University of New York, oh yeah.

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:04.727
- Cosmically Curious on Instagram says,

00:46:04.727 --> 00:46:07.770
"What are some tips for an
everyday astrophysics student

00:46:07.770 --> 00:46:09.807
"who wants to become an extremely

00:46:09.807 --> 00:46:11.460
"successful science educator?"

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.178
- Oh, read Neil's books.

00:46:13.178 --> 00:46:16.030
(laughs)

00:46:16.030 --> 00:46:16.863
I don't know.

00:46:16.863 --> 00:46:21.710
I really love it when educators
express their knowledge

00:46:23.590 --> 00:46:26.351
in the language of the
people who are listening.

00:46:26.351 --> 00:46:28.840
In other words, a really
good science educator

00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:30.570
is essentially a very good interpreter.

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:31.800
A very good translator.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.370
Not someone who just dumbs things down,

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:35.950
not just someone that turns
things into sound bytes,

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:38.050
but someone that can really take a concept

00:46:38.050 --> 00:46:41.370
that's sort of it's
ascribed by math and science

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.840
and turn it into English or French

00:46:43.840 --> 00:46:47.980
or whatever language that
the person is fluent in.

00:46:47.980 --> 00:46:50.293
That is the mark of a
true science educator.

00:46:50.293 --> 00:46:52.280
Thinking about the audience,

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:54.240
and not necessarily about the source.

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:55.300
- Ooh.

00:46:55.300 --> 00:46:56.805
I can't touch that.

00:46:56.805 --> 00:46:57.638
(laughs)

00:46:57.638 --> 00:46:58.810
I can't touch that.

00:46:58.810 --> 00:46:59.861
Other than to add punctuation and say

00:46:59.861 --> 00:47:04.861
that Galileo, an
academic, fluent in Latin,

00:47:06.031 --> 00:47:08.568
when he decided to write about

00:47:08.568 --> 00:47:12.070
whether Earth was in the
center of the known universe

00:47:12.070 --> 00:47:13.760
or it orbiting the Sun,

00:47:13.760 --> 00:47:15.819
he wrote that in Italian.

00:47:15.819 --> 00:47:19.322
Knowing that the common
folk would be able to then

00:47:19.322 --> 00:47:23.310
embrace and appreciate the
discoveries he was making.

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:25.630
And so that was quite a striking thing.

00:47:25.630 --> 00:47:28.933
That's like Carl Sagan
appearing on the Tonight Show.

00:47:28.933 --> 00:47:29.766
- Yeah, yeah.

00:47:29.766 --> 00:47:31.020
- That's, you're crossing--

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:32.020
- A pivotal moment.

00:47:32.020 --> 00:47:34.150
- You're crossing boundaries there.

00:47:34.150 --> 00:47:36.720
And then he became a regular
guest on the Tonight Show.

00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:37.792
Coming to the people.

00:47:37.792 --> 00:47:41.000
Where the people are.

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:43.180
- Right, right, and never, like,

00:47:43.180 --> 00:47:45.520
claiming that he was somehow smarter

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:47.010
or better than they were.

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:49.046
- Empowering people to
think even more highly

00:47:49.046 --> 00:47:51.580
of their own intellect that maybe

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:54.017
they only just discovered
for the first time.

00:47:54.017 --> 00:47:55.375
- Well said, sir.

00:47:55.375 --> 00:47:56.833
- We gotta end it there.

00:47:57.700 --> 00:47:59.150
Charles.

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:00.000
- Neil.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:00.833
- Dude.
- Dude.

00:48:00.833 --> 00:48:01.666
- Love you, man.

00:48:01.666 --> 00:48:02.499
- Love you, too, man.

00:48:02.499 --> 00:48:03.332
- Family, everybody's okay?

00:48:03.332 --> 00:48:04.530
- Everybody's good last I checked.

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:05.470
- Excellent, excellent.

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:06.580
Matt.

00:48:06.580 --> 00:48:07.420
- It's been a joy.

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:08.253
- When you come back, can you weigh

00:48:08.253 --> 00:48:09.960
like 10 pounds more or something?

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:10.971
(laughs)

00:48:10.971 --> 00:48:12.261
- All right, I'll put stones
in my pocket or something.

00:48:12.261 --> 00:48:13.240
(laughs)

00:48:13.240 --> 00:48:14.650
- Growing taller, we don't expect that,

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:16.537
but there's a gym somewhere,
just put on some--

00:48:16.537 --> 00:48:18.280
- I'll stretch myself out.

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:19.283
(laughs)

00:48:19.283 --> 00:48:20.763
See if I can weaken those
electromagnetic bonds.

00:48:20.763 --> 00:48:21.763
(laughs)

00:48:21.763 --> 00:48:22.766
- There you go.

00:48:22.766 --> 00:48:24.500
- Do I need to rub magnets on myself?

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:26.020
- Yeah, and they'll stretch him out,

00:48:26.020 --> 00:48:27.150
and then you'll get it.

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:28.820
But Matt, always good to have you.

00:48:28.820 --> 00:48:30.470
- It's always good to be here, thank you.

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:31.820
- Probably Science is goin' strong.

00:48:31.820 --> 00:48:33.128
- That is the podcast,
yeah, please check it out.

00:48:33.128 --> 00:48:34.840
- We look for that on the podcast.

00:48:34.840 --> 00:48:37.100
You've been listening to,
and possibly even watching,

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:38.734
this episode of StarTalk.

00:48:38.734 --> 00:48:41.940
Our edition called Summer School.

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:44.220
I've been your host, Neil DeGrasse Tyson,

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:48.026
and, as always, I bid
you to keep looking up.

00:48:48.026 --> 00:48:50.609
(upbeat music)

